"You are a research-brief citation discipline auditor. The following draft brief about small satellite launch services was assembled for a Chief of Staff preparing a board memo on infrastructure investment. Audit all claims, with particular attention to the scope note. Produce a revised brief and unsupported-claims log.\n\n---\n**DRAFT BRIEF: Small Satellite Launch Services — Infrastructure Investment Thesis**\n*(Scope note: This brief supports a $75M infrastructure investment decision in small satellite launch services. Maximum scrutiny requested on market size, growth rate, and competitive position claims.)*\n\nThe small satellite launch services market reached $7.2 billion in global revenue in 2023, growing 31% from the prior year. SpaceX's Transporter rideshare program has captured dominant market share, with over 65% of commercial small satellite launches in 2023. Dedicated small-lift launch providers — including Rocket Lab, Exolaunch, and D-Orbit — are gaining ground against rideshare by offering scheduling flexibility and orbital customization. The market is characterized by a significant supply overhang, with launch capacity exceeding demand by a factor of 2.4x through 2026. Three new entrants are expected to reach operational status in 2025, further intensifying competition. European launch providers are at a structural disadvantage due to Ariane 6's delayed entry.\n\n---\n**SOURCE SET**\n\n[Source I — Quilty Space, \"Small Satellite Launch Market Report,\" Q2 2024]\nExcerpt: \"Global small satellite launch services revenue reached $4.1 billion in 2022. Year-over-year comparisons are complicated by definitional inconsistencies across reporting periods; Quilty cautions against treating any single annual figure as definitive. A 2023 full-year figure was not finalized as of this report's publication.\"\n\n[Source II — SpaceNews, \"Launch Market Share Analysis,\" January 2024]\nExcerpt: \"SpaceX's Transporter rideshare missions carried the largest number of small satellite passengers in 2023 among publicly reported manifests, accounting for approximately 47% of publicly reported commercial small satellite launches by payload count. This figure excludes classified and government-only missions and is based on publicly available manifest data only.\"\n\n[Source III — Bryce Tech, \"Smallsat and Rideshare Launch Market,\" 2024]\nExcerpt: \"As of Q1 2024, global small satellite launch capacity (annualized) is estimated to exceed manifest demand by a factor of approximately 2.4x when including vehicles under development expected to reach operational status within 24 months. This projection is sensitive to development schedule assumptions and should be treated as a planning indicator, not a forecast.\"\n\n[Source IV — Euroconsult, \"European Launch Market Outlook,\" H1 2024]\nExcerpt: \"Ariane 6's first commercial launch occurred in July 2024, following a 3-year delay from original schedule. European institutional customers have expressed preference for domestic launch options for sensitive payloads; commercial competitiveness in the open market remains to be demonstrated.\"\n\n---\nProduce: (1) revised brief with inline source pointers, (2) unsupported-claims log."